onal ↘\n\n    Extending: next would be J6, then K5. Are they occupied? J6: empty. So Black could go toward J6 — but not immediate.\n\n    But more importantly, what about White’s opportunities?\n\nBack to White: I8, J8, K8 are O. Is there a way to form five?\n\nWait — is there a **vertical** or **other diagonal**?\n\nCheck vertical at J8: J7=., J8=O, J9=. → no.\n\nDiagonals through J8:\n- ↗ (A15-O1): I9, J8, K7 → I9=X, J8=O, K7=. → not useful.\n- ↘ (A1-O15): I7=., H8=X, G9=X → mixed.\n\nSo best line is still horizontal at row 8.\n\nNow: can we play **L8** to make I8-K8-L8 = four white stones?\n\nYes: I8(O), J8(O), K8(O), L8(empty). So playing L8 creates a 4-in-a-row.\n\nBut not a win yet.\n\nHowever — is there a **move that creates two threats**, one of which is unstoppable?\n\nAlternatively, look for **an open three that can become a winning four with fork**?\n\nWait — what about **J10**? No, not relevant.\n\nAnother idea: **K9**?\n\nLet’s check K9: currently empty? Yes. Not in occupied list.\n\nIf White plays K9:\n- K9 vs existing: G9(X), I9(X), so K9 would be isolated unless connected otherwise.\n\nNo.\n\nWait — what about **J7**?\n\nJ7: currently empty.\n\nJ7 would be below J8(O). Vertical: J7, J8(O), J9(.) — J9 is empty. But J9 is surrounded by Xs? I9=X, K9=?, but not helpful.\n\nBut consider: is there a **double threat** move?\n\nWait — critical insight: White already has **three in a row**: I8, J8, K8.\n\nIf we play **L8**, we get four in a row: I8-L8.\n\nThat is a **direct threat**: next turn, playing M8 would win. But opponent will block.\n\nBut if we can create a **two-way threat**, that would be better.\n\nIs there a square that completes a 5 or sets up an unblockable 5?\n\nWait — perhaps **we can win immediately**?\n\nIs there any five-in-a-row already? No.\n\nBut let’s re-express: can White play a stone that forms **five consecutive O\'s**?\n\nOnly if there is a line of four O\'s with an empty end — but we only have three O\'s in a row.\n\nSo no immediate win.\n\nBut — what about **creating an open four**?\n\nBest candidate: **L8**.\n\nAfter L8: I8-O, J8-O, K8-O, L8-O → four in a row, and M8 is free → open on the right. Left is blocked by H8=X.\n\nSo it\'s a **half-open four**, but still very strong — Black must respond to block at M8.\n\nBut is there a better move?\n\nWhat about **H8**? Already taken by Black.\n\nAnother direction: diagonal?\n\nCheck if White can form a diagonal.\n\nFor example: H7, I8, J9, K10... H7 is empty? Yes. I8=O, J9=?, K10=?\n\nJ9 is empty, but not aligned with others.\n\nOr: J8, I9 — I9 is X, enemy.\n\nNo.\n\nWait — another idea: **K7**?\n\nK7: empty. Adjacent to K8(O). Diagonally: K7, J8, I9 — J8=O, I9=X → not good.\n\nNo.\n\nBack to L8: strongest move.\n\nBut wait — what about **L9**? No connection.\n\nAlternatively, is there a **fork** move?\n\nWait — consider **L8**: it extends the horizontal to four.\n\nBut also, does L8 participate in any other line?\n\nVertical L8: L7=., L8=O, L9=., etc. → no.\n\nDiagonal: K7, L8 → K7=?, L8=O → weak.\n\nSo only one threat.\n\nBut still, creating a four-in-a-row is forcing — Black must block.\n\nBut can we instead create a **double threat**?\n\nWait — is there a move that creates both a four and a separate three?\n\nNot obvious.\n\nAlternatively, consider **J10**? No.\n\nWait — what about **I6**? Too far.\n\nPerhaps **defensive check**: does Black have a threat?\n\nBlack has:\n- G9(X), H8(X), I7(X) — diagonal ↘ from G9 to I7: three in a row.\n  - Next: J6, K5 — both empty. So Black could play J6 to make four?\n  - G9, H8, I7, J6 — all X? Yes, possible.\n  - So Black has a potential diagonal four.\n\nBut J6 is empty — so if we don’t block, Black could play J6 → then threaten K5 to complete five.\n\nBut is that immediate? No — because even after J6, it’s only four, and we can block K5.\n\nBut more urgent: our own attack.\n\nWhite’s I8-J8-K8 is strong — we should extend it.\n\nBut can we play **L8** safely?\n\nYes — and it creates a four-in-a-row thre