mpty. Let\'s see diagonals.\n\nCheck diagonal from G9 (X) → H8 (X) → I7 (X): that’s three X’s in a diagonal (G9-H8-I7), going from bottom-left to top-right. That’s a dangerous black threat! It’s a diagonal: G9, H8, I7 — all X. That’s three in a row, and it can extend:\n- Forward: J6, K5...\n- Backward: F10, E11...\n\nCurrently, G9 (X), H8 (X), I7 (X) — that’s three in a row for Black (X), and if Black plays J6 next, it becomes four. Even worse, it’s open on both ends? Let’s check:\n\n- Back: F10 — empty\n- Forward: J6 — empty\n\nSo Black has a **three-in-a-row diagonal threat**, which could become a four, then five. This is a serious danger.\n\nBut it\'s our turn — we are White. We must prevent Black from forming a long open sequence.\n\nHowever, is there a winning move for us?\n\nLet’s look again at White’s position.\n\nWhite has: H8 is X — no, H8 is X. Wait — correction from board:\n\nFrom the board:\n- H8: X\n- I8: O\n- J8: O\n- K8: O\n- L8: O\n\nSo the white stones are I8, J8, K8, L8 — four in a row, but:\n- Left: H8 is X → blocked\n- Right: M8 is X → blocked\n\nSo this four is dead. Cannot be extended.\n\nAre there other white stones? Only G9 is X, I9 is X — no other O\'s listed except I8, J8, K8, L8.\n\nWait — the list says: "Occupied Coordinates: I7, H8, I8, J8, K8, L8, M8, G9, I9"\n\nAnd symbols:\n- I7: X\n- H8: X\n- I8: O\n- J8: O\n- K8: O\n- L8: O\n- M8: X\n- G9: X\n- I9: X\n\nSo White only has four stones: I8, J8, K8, L8 — all in a blocked horizontal line.\n\nWe need to expand.\n\nBest strategy: create a new threat, ideally a fork or open three.\n\nConsider playing at **J9** — currently empty.\n\nJ9 is adjacent to:\n- Above: J10\n- Below: J8 (O)\n- Left: I9 (X)\n- Right: K9\n- Diagonals: I8 (O), K8 (O)\n\nJ9 is diagonally connected to I8 and K8, and vertically to J8.\n\nIf we play at J9:\n- Vertical: J8 (O), J9 (new O) — only two\n- Horizontal: I9 (X), J9 (O), K9 (?) — K9 is empty, but I9 is X, so left blocked\n- Diagonal: from H7 to I8 to J9 to K10 — currently: I8 (O), J9 (O) — could be start\n- Other diagonal: from K8 to J9 to I10 — K8 (O), J9 (O) — two in a row\n\nNot immediately powerful.\n\nWhat about **H9**? Empty.\n\nH9:\n- Left: G9 (X)\n- Right: I9 (X) → so H9 is between two X’s horizontally — bad\n- Up: H10\n- Down: H8 (X)\n- Diagonals: G8 (empty), I8 (O), G10, I10\n\nI8 is O, so diagonal G9-H8-I7 is all X, but H9 to I8 is diagonal down-right? No.\n\nDiagonal from H9 to I8 to J7: H9 (if O), I8 (O), J7 (?) — that’s a diagonal.\n\nJ7 is currently empty.\n\nSo if we play H9, then we have:\n- H9 (O), I8 (O), J7 (?) — if J7 is played later, could be three.\n\nBut J7 is currently empty.\n\nBut more importantly: recall the Black threat: G9 (X), H8 (X), I7 (X) — diagonal up-right: G9, H8, I7 — all X.\n\nThis line continues to J6, K5, etc.\n\nIt is currently three in a row, open on both ends.\n\nIf Black gets to play J6, it becomes four-in-a-row with open ends — very dangerous.\n\nSo as White, we should consider **blocking** this threat.\n\nBest blocking move: play at **J6** or **F10**.\n\n- J6 is forward extension\n- F10 is backward extension\n\nWhich is more urgent?\n\nThe cluster of stones is around columns G-I, rows 7-9. So J6 is closer to the center of activity than F10.\n\nAlso, J6 is adjacent to I7 and K5, so likely more critical.\n\nMoreover, if we don’t block, Black could play J6 next and create a four-in-a-row threat.\n\nBut we are White — can we create a stronger threat?\n\nWait — what if we play **M9**?\n\nNo support.\n\nAlternatively: what about **L9**?\n\nL9:\n- Left: K9\n- Right: M9\n- Up: L10\n- Down: L8 (O)\n- Diagonal: K8 (O), M8 (X)\n\nK8 (O), L9 (if O), M10 — not helpful.\n\nOr diagonal: K9, L9, M9 — no.\n\nBut L8 is O, so vertical: L8 (O), L9 (new O) — only two.\n\nStill weak.\n\nAnother idea: **J10**?\n\nToo far.\n\nWait — reconsider the diagonal threat from Black: G9-H8-I7-J6-K5.\n\nTo block it effectively, we can play at **J6** — that would break the sequence.\n\nJ6 is currently empty.\n\nIf 